{
  "id": "section-present-past",
  "moduleId": "verb-tenses",
  "label": "Section 1",
  "title": "Present and Past",
  "summary": "Common present and past endings, plus the topic and object particles that appear in everyday sentences.",
  "learningObjectives": [
    "Form the casual present (-아/어/해) and past (-았어/었어/했어) of common verbs",
    "Recognize the topic (은/는), subject (이/가), and object (을/를) particles",
    "Read and say short sentences about eating, drinking, studying, and liking"
  ],
  "newVocabularyIds": ["eat", "drink", "study", "like", "love", "rice-meal", "water", "korean-language", "i-me", "you-casual"],
  "grammarConceptIds": ["word-order-sov", "topic-eun-neun", "subject-i-ga", "object-eul-reul", "present-casual", "past-casual"],
  "estimatedMinutes": 20,
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "s1-intro",
      "type": "intro",
      "title": "Talking about what you do and did",
      "body": [
        "Korean verbs are listed in their dictionary form, which always ends in -다: 먹다 (to eat), 마시다 (to drink), 공부하다 (to study).",
        "To use a verb in a sentence, remove 다 to find the stem, then add an ending. The ending shows the tense and how polite you are being.",
        "In this section you will learn ten everyday words and use them in present-tense sentences (먹어, I eat) and past-tense sentences (먹었어, I ate)."
      ],
      "callout": "All sentences in this module use casual speech (반말, banmal): the style for close friends and people younger than you. Polite speech, which adds 요, comes in a later module."
    },
    {
      "id": "s1-vocab-verbs",
      "type": "vocabulary",
      "title": "New words: five verbs",
      "intro": "Each verb is shown in its dictionary form. You'll use all five in sentences in a moment.",
      "vocabularyIds": ["eat", "drink", "study", "like", "love"]
    },
    {
      "id": "s1-vocab-nouns",
      "type": "vocabulary",
      "title": "New words: people and things",
      "intro": "These nouns and pronouns are the who and the what for our first sentences.",
      "vocabularyIds": ["rice-meal", "water", "korean-language", "i-me", "you-casual"]
    },
    {
      "id": "s1-grammar-word-order",
      "type": "grammar",
      "title": "Sentence order and particles",
      "grammarConceptIds": ["word-order-sov", "topic-eun-neun", "subject-i-ga", "object-eul-reul"],
      "body": [
        "A basic Korean sentence puts the verb last: the topic or subject comes first, then the object, then the verb.",
        "Small particles attach to the end of nouns to show their role in the sentence. Each particle has two forms, chosen by whether the noun ends in a vowel or a consonant.",
        "In everyday conversation, particles and even the subject are often left out when the meaning is clear. Lessons show full sentences first so the structure stays visible."
      ],
      "rules": [
        { "pattern": "은/는: topic", "explanation": "What the sentence is about. 나 + 는 → 나는 (as for me)" },
        { "pattern": "이/가: subject", "explanation": "Who or what does the action. 친구 + 가 → 친구가. You'll meet it in Section 2." },
        { "pattern": "을/를: object", "explanation": "What receives the action. 밥 + 을 → 밥을, 너 + 를 → 너를" }
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "id": "s1-order-demo",
          "korean": "나는 한국어를 좋아해.",
          "romanization": "Naneun hangugeoreul joahae.",
          "english": "I like Korean.",
          "literal": "I (topic) · Korean (object) · like",
          "tokens": [
            { "text": "나는", "romanization": "naneun", "meaning": "I (topic)", "role": "topic", "marker": "는", "vocabularyId": "i-me", "grammarConceptId": "topic-eun-neun" },
            { "text": "한국어를", "romanization": "hangugeoreul", "meaning": "Korean (object)", "role": "object", "marker": "를", "vocabularyId": "korean-language", "grammarConceptId": "object-eul-reul" },
            { "text": "좋아해", "romanization": "joahae", "meaning": "like", "role": "verb", "vocabularyId": "like", "grammarConceptId": "present-casual" }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "callout": {
        "title": "Vowel or consonant?",
        "body": [
          "Look at the last sound of the noun. 나 and 너 end in a vowel → 나는, 너를.",
          "밥 ends in the consonant ㅂ and 물 ends in ㄹ → 밥을, 물을."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s1-grammar-present",
      "type": "grammar",
      "title": "Present tense: -아/어/해",
      "grammarConceptIds": ["present-casual"],
      "body": [
        "Remove 다 from the dictionary form to find the stem: 먹다 → 먹, 마시다 → 마시, 공부하다 → 공부하.",
        "If the stem's last vowel is ㅏ or ㅗ, add 아. Otherwise add 어. Every 하다 verb becomes 해.",
        "The present tense covers habits and general facts (I eat rice, I study Korean) and what is happening around now."
      ],
      "rules": [
        { "pattern": "stem with ㅏ/ㅗ + 아", "explanation": "You'll practice these stems in Section 2 (가 → 가, 오 → 와)." },
        { "pattern": "other stems + 어", "explanation": "먹 + 어 → 먹어, 마시 + 어 → 마셔" },
        { "pattern": "하다 → 해", "explanation": "공부하다 → 공부해, 좋아하다 → 좋아해" }
      ],
      "forms": [
        { "dictionary": "먹다", "vocabularyId": "eat", "form": "먹어", "romanization": "meogeo", "english": "eat", "parts": [{ "text": "먹", "kind": "stem" }, { "text": "어", "kind": "ending" }] },
        { "dictionary": "마시다", "vocabularyId": "drink", "form": "마셔", "romanization": "masyeo", "english": "drink", "parts": [{ "text": "마셔", "kind": "contracted" }], "derivation": "마시 + 어 → 마셔 (ㅣ and ㅓ merge into ㅕ)" },
        { "dictionary": "공부하다", "vocabularyId": "study", "form": "공부해", "romanization": "gongbuhae", "english": "study", "parts": [{ "text": "공부", "kind": "stem" }, { "text": "해", "kind": "ending" }], "derivation": "하다 → 해" },
        { "dictionary": "좋아하다", "vocabularyId": "like", "form": "좋아해", "romanization": "joahae", "english": "like", "parts": [{ "text": "좋아", "kind": "stem" }, { "text": "해", "kind": "ending" }], "derivation": "하다 → 해" },
        { "dictionary": "사랑하다", "vocabularyId": "love", "form": "사랑해", "romanization": "saranghae", "english": "love", "parts": [{ "text": "사랑", "kind": "stem" }, { "text": "해", "kind": "ending" }], "derivation": "하다 → 해" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "s1-examples-present",
      "type": "example",
      "title": "Present-tense sentences",
      "intro": "Tap any word to see its breakdown.",
      "sentences": [
        {
          "id": "s1-eat-present",
          "korean": "나는 밥을 먹어.",
          "romanization": "Naneun babeul meogeo.",
          "english": "I eat a meal.",
          "literal": "I (topic) · meal (object) · eat",
          "tokens": [
            { "text": "나는", "romanization": "naneun", "meaning": "I (topic)", "role": "topic", "marker": "는", "vocabularyId": "i-me", "grammarConceptId": "topic-eun-neun" },
            { "text": "밥을", "romanization": "babeul", "meaning": "meal (object)", "role": "object", "marker": "을", "vocabularyId": "rice-meal", "grammarConceptId": "object-eul-reul" },
            { "text": "먹어", "romanization": "meogeo", "meaning": "eat", "role": "verb", "marker": "어", "vocabularyId": "eat", "grammarConceptId": "present-casual" }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "s1-drink-present",
          "korean": "나는 물을 마셔.",
          "romanization": "Naneun mureul masyeo.",
          "english": "I drink water.",
          "tokens": [
            { "text": "나는", "romanization": "naneun", "meaning": "I (topic)", "role": "topic", "marker": "는", "vocabularyId": "i-me", "grammarConceptId": "topic-eun-neun" },
            { "text": "물을", "romanization": "mureul", "meaning": "water (object)", "role": "object", "marker": "을", "vocabularyId": "water", "grammarConceptId": "object-eul-reul" },
            { "text": "마셔", "romanization": "masyeo", "meaning": "drink", "role": "verb", "vocabularyId": "drink", "grammarConceptId": "present-casual" }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "s1-study-present",
          "korean": "나는 한국어를 공부해.",
          "romanization": "Naneun hangugeoreul gongbuhae.",
          "english": "I study Korean.",
          "tokens": [
            { "text": "나는", "romanization": "naneun", "meaning": "I (topic)", "role": "topic", "marker": "는", "vocabularyId": "i-me", "grammarConceptId": "topic-eun-neun" },
            { "text": "한국어를", "romanization": "hangugeoreul", "meaning": "Korean (object)", "role": "object", "marker": "를", "vocabularyId": "korean-language", "grammarConceptId": "object-eul-reul" },
            { "text": "공부해", "romanization": "gongbuhae", "meaning": "study", "role": "verb", "marker": "해", "vocabularyId": "study", "grammarConceptId": "present-casual" }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "s1-like-present",
          "korean": "나는 너를 좋아해.",
          "romanization": "Naneun neoreul joahae.",
          "english": "I like you.",
          "tokens": [
            { "text": "나는", "romanization": "naneun", "meaning": "I (topic)", "role": "topic", "marker": "는", "vocabularyId": "i-me", "grammarConceptId": "topic-eun-neun" },
            { "text": "너를", "romanization": "neoreul", "meaning": "you (object)", "role": "object", "marker": "를", "vocabularyId": "you-casual", "grammarConceptId": "object-eul-reul" },
            { "text": "좋아해", "romanization": "joahae", "meaning": "like", "role": "verb", "marker": "해", "vocabularyId": "like", "grammarConceptId": "present-casual" }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "s1-love-present",
          "korean": "나는 너를 사랑해.",
          "romanization": "Naneun neoreul saranghae.",
          "english": "I love you.",
          "note": "사랑해 on its own is a very common way to say \"I love you.\"",
          "tokens": [
            { "text": "나는", "romanization": "naneun", "meaning": "I (topic)", "role": "topic", "marker": "는", "vocabularyId": "i-me", "grammarConceptId": "topic-eun-neun" },
            { "text": "너를", "romanization": "neoreul", "meaning": "you (object)", "role": "object", "marker": "를", "vocabularyId": "you-casual", "grammarConceptId": "object-eul-reul" },
            { "text": "사랑해", "romanization": "saranghae", "meaning": "love", "role": "verb", "marker": "해", "vocabularyId": "love", "grammarConceptId": "present-casual" }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "Every sentence ends with the verb, and every object ends in 을 or 를."
    },
    {
      "id": "s1-check-present",
      "type": "quiz",
      "title": "Quick check: present tense",
      "questions": [
        {
          "id": "q-s1-present-drink",
          "type": "multipleChoice",
          "prompt": "What is the casual present form of 마시다 (to drink)?",
          "options": [
            { "id": "a", "text": "마셔" },
            { "id": "b", "text": "마셨어" },
            { "id": "c", "text": "마시다" }
          ],
          "correctOptionId": "a",
          "explanation": "마시 + 어 contracts to 마셔 (masyeo). 마셨어 is the past tense, and 마시다 is the dictionary form, which isn't used as-is in conversation.",
          "conceptIds": ["present-casual"],
          "vocabularyIds": ["drink"]
        },
        {
          "id": "q-s1-present-like",
          "type": "multipleChoice",
          "prompt": "What does 좋아하다 (to like) become in the casual present?",
          "options": [
            { "id": "a", "text": "좋아어" },
            { "id": "b", "text": "좋아해" },
            { "id": "c", "text": "좋아하어" }
          ],
          "correctOptionId": "b",
          "explanation": "Every 하다 verb becomes 해: 좋아하다 → 좋아해 (joahae).",
          "hint": "Look at the last part of the verb: 하다.",
          "conceptIds": ["present-casual"],
          "vocabularyIds": ["like"]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "s1-grammar-past",
      "type": "grammar",
      "title": "Past tense: -았어/었어/했어",
      "grammarConceptIds": ["past-casual"],
      "body": [
        "The past tense follows the same vowel rule as the present: ㅏ/ㅗ stems take 았어, other stems take 었어, and 하다 becomes 했어.",
        "A shortcut: take the present form, add ㅆ under its last syllable, then add 어. 먹어 → 먹었어, 마셔 → 마셨어, 공부해 → 공부했어."
      ],
      "rules": [
        { "pattern": "other stems + 었어", "explanation": "먹 + 었어 → 먹었어, 마시 + 었어 → 마셨어" },
        { "pattern": "하다 → 했어", "explanation": "공부하다 → 공부했어, 사랑하다 → 사랑했어" }
      ],
      "forms": [
        { "dictionary": "먹다", "vocabularyId": "eat", "form": "먹었어", "romanization": "meogeosseo", "english": "ate", "parts": [{ "text": "먹", "kind": "stem" }, { "text": "었어", "kind": "ending" }] },
        { "dictionary": "마시다", "vocabularyId": "drink", "form": "마셨어", "romanization": "masyeosseo", "english": "drank", "parts": [{ "text": "마셨어", "kind": "contracted" }], "derivation": "마시 + 었어 → 마셨어" },
        { "dictionary": "공부하다", "vocabularyId": "study", "form": "공부했어", "romanization": "gongbuhaesseo", "english": "studied", "parts": [{ "text": "공부", "kind": "stem" }, { "text": "했어", "kind": "ending" }], "derivation": "하다 → 했어" },
        { "dictionary": "좋아하다", "vocabularyId": "like", "form": "좋아했어", "romanization": "joahaesseo", "english": "liked", "parts": [{ "text": "좋아", "kind": "stem" }, { "text": "했어", "kind": "ending" }], "derivation": "하다 → 했어" },
        { "dictionary": "사랑하다", "vocabularyId": "love", "form": "사랑했어", "romanization": "saranghaesseo", "english": "loved", "parts": [{ "text": "사랑", "kind": "stem" }, { "text": "했어", "kind": "ending" }], "derivation": "하다 → 했어" }
      ],
      "callout": {
        "title": "좋아했어 vs. 좋아한다고 했어",
        "body": [
          "좋아했어 (joahaesseo) means \"liked\": 나는 너를 좋아했어. = I liked you.",
          "좋아한다고 했어 (joahandago haesseo) means \"said that (someone) likes.\" There, 했어 is the past tense of saying, and the liking stays in the present. You don't need this pattern yet; just don't mix the two up."
        ]
      }
    },
